From: PS4 port
Subject: [PATCH] orbis: turn fastmem off in the setting the emitter actually reads

⚠ THIS FINISHES PATCH 0006, WHICH FIXED THE WRONG VARIABLE. That patch cleared Bus's copy of the
fastmem mode and re-derived g_state.fastmem_base from it - which set the base to null, the state
it was already in - and left g_settings.cpu_fastmem_mode at LUT. The recompiler asks g_settings,
not Bus, so it went on emitting fastmem code. The crash came back unchanged:

    fatal: signal 11 - SIGSEGV, si_code 1, fault address 0
    fatal: rip 0000000275ecf801  rsp 00000007eeffa9c8  addr 0000000000000000
    fatal: rip 0x275ecf801 is in 0x275ecc000-0x278ecc000 prot 0x7 (rwx) flexible, committed
    fatal: code 0x275ecf7f1: bf 3c 09 00 00 89 fe c1 ef 0c 81 e6 ff 0f 00 00
    fatal: code 0x275ecf801 (rip in this row): 48 8b 3c fb 44 8b 24 37 41 bd 3c 09 00 00 41 83

Those bytes are cpu_recompiler_code_generator_x64.cpp:1725-1743, the LUT arm of
EmitLoadGuestRAMFastmem, instruction for instruction:

    mov  edi, 0x93C          EmitCopyValue(RARG1, address)   - a CONSTANT guest address
    mov  esi, edi
    shr  edi, 12             LUT page index
    and  esi, 0xFFF          HOST_PAGE_OFFSET_MASK
    mov  rdi, [rbx + rdi*8]  <- faulted.  RMEMBASEPTR is RBX (x64.cpp:13)
    mov  r12d, [rdi + rsi]

RBX is loaded once per block by EmitBeginBlock:

    m_emit->mov(GetFastmemBasePtrReg(), qword[GetCPUPtrReg() + offsetof(CPU::State, fastmem_base)]);

so a null RBX means g_state.fastmem_base was null when the block RAN, and the block was emitted
anyway because g_settings said fastmem was on when it was COMPILED. Both halves have one cause.

THE CHAIN, IN ORDER:

 1. common/page_fault_handler.cpp defines USE_SIGSEGV only for __linux__ / __ANDROID__ /
    __APPLE__ / __FreeBSD__. This SDK's libc++ undefines __FreeBSD__ in every C++ TU and nothing
    names __ORBIS__, so InstallHandler() reaches `#else return false;` - unconditionally, on this
    platform, with no way for it to succeed. The log has said so since patch 0003:

        [InitializeFastmem] Failed to install page fault handler

 2. CPU::CodeCache::Initialize() runs `if (g_settings.IsUsingFastmem() && !InitializeFastmem()) {}`
    BEFORE CompileDispatcher and before any block is compiled. InitializeFastmem returns early on
    that failure, so Bus::UpdateFastmemViews(mode) never runs and m_fastmem_lut - a 16 MiB calloc
    at bus.cpp:400 - is NEVER ALLOCATED. It did not fail; it was never asked for.

 3. Bus::GetFastmemBase() returns nullptr while Bus's mode is Disabled, so
    CPU::UpdateFastmemBase() leaves g_state.fastmem_base null. That is the value RBX gets.

 4. g_settings.cpu_fastmem_mode is still LUT (the libretro option's default), so
    IsUsingFastmem() is still true and generic.cpp:40 keeps emitting EmitLoadGuestRAMFastmem for
    every constant RAM address. ⚠ That call site is gated ONLY by IsUsingFastmem() - not by
    cpu_fastmem_rewrite, which libretro_host_interface.cpp:1079 already forces false in LUT mode.
    It is the one fastmem emitter still live here, which is why every reported fault has been a
    constant address.

⚠ AND FASTMEM CANNOT BE MADE TO WORK BY ALLOCATING THE LUT EITHER. In LUT mode the handler is not
an optimisation, it is the mechanism: a null LUT entry for a non-RAM address is how a fastmem
access is turned into a slowmem one (LUTPageFaultHandler backpatches it), and
CPU::UpdateFastmemBase() deliberately nulls the base whenever cop0 SR.Isc is set, trusting the
same handler to catch the result. Without a handler both of those are crashes.

So the fix is to say so in the setting the emitter reads, at the point upstream reserves for
exactly this - HostInterface::FixIncompatibleSettings, two lines below the identical downgrade
`#ifndef WITH_MMAP_FASTMEM` already performs. It runs on the initial LoadSettings and on every
core-option change (libretro_host_interface.cpp:366 and :1350), both before the code cache is
(re)initialised, so no block is ever compiled with it on.

⚠ THE `shr 12` / `and 0xFFF` IN THAT DISASSEMBLY ARE NOT A 4096 ASSUMPTION ABOUT THE HOST.
HOST_PAGE_SIZE (core/types.h:187) is swanstation's own LUT granularity over the 4 GiB guest
window - FASTMEM_LUT_NUM_PAGES is 0x100000000 >> 12 - and in LUT mode it never reaches mprotect.
The console's 16 KiB granule, which patches 0004 and 0005 exist for, is not involved. The one
place HOST_PAGE_SIZE does reach SetPageProtection is bus.cpp:357/493, inside the MMap arm, which
patch 0001 already made unreachable here.

Left for later: an __ORBIS__ arm in page_fault_handler.cpp. orbis-compat now delivers SA_SIGINFO
with FreeBSD's value, and this handler only rewrites code and returns - it never edits the
ucontext, which this kernel ignores anyway - so it is plausible. It is also a performance
project that needs hardware to iterate on, and it is not what makes the core run.

--- a/src/core/cpu_code_cache.cpp
+++ b/src/core/cpu_code_cache.cpp
@@ -1019,6 +1019,16 @@
     //
     // Disabling it here is the difference between "fastmem is unavailable" and "fastmem is
     // unavailable and the generated code does not know".
+    //
+    // ⚠ AND THE SETTING IS THE VARIABLE THAT MATTERS - the two lines below it were not enough.
+    // The emitter never looks at Bus; it asks g_settings.IsUsingFastmem(), which is
+    // `cpu_fastmem_mode != Disabled`. Clearing Bus's copy only made GetFastmemBase() return
+    // nullptr, which is precisely the null RBX the generated code then indexed the LUT through.
+    // In practice this whole branch is now dead - FixIncompatibleSettings turns the mode off
+    // before System::Boot ever reaches here - but leaving it wrong would put the crash one
+    // settings reload away.
+    g_settings.cpu_fastmem_mode = CPUFastmemMode::Disabled;
+    g_settings.cpu_fastmem_rewrite = false;
     Bus::UpdateFastmemViews(CPUFastmemMode::Disabled);
     CPU::UpdateFastmemBase();
 #endif
diff --git a/src/core/host_interface.cpp b/src/core/host_interface.cpp
index 1d6f503..c9c0225 100644
--- a/src/core/host_interface.cpp
+++ b/src/core/host_interface.cpp
@@ -211,6 +211,46 @@ void HostInterface::FixIncompatibleSettings(bool display_osd_messages)
   }
 #endif
 
+#ifdef __ORBIS__
+  // ⚠ EVERY FASTMEM MODE NEEDS A PAGE-FAULT HANDLER, AND THIS PLATFORM CANNOT INSTALL ONE.
+  // common/page_fault_handler.cpp only defines USE_SIGSEGV for __linux__/__ANDROID__/__APPLE__/
+  // __FreeBSD__, and this SDK's libc++ undefines __FreeBSD__ in every C++ TU, so InstallHandler
+  // falls through to `#else return false;` unconditionally. The log says so:
+  //
+  //     [InitializeFastmem] Failed to install page fault handler
+  //
+  // Turning the mode off HERE is what actually stops the recompiler, because the emitter asks
+  // g_settings - not Bus - whether to emit fastmem accesses:
+  //
+  //     generic.cpp:40   if (g_settings.IsUsingFastmem() && Bus::IsRAMAddress(...))
+  //                        EmitLoadGuestRAMFastmem(...)
+  //
+  // and IsUsingFastmem() is `cpu_fastmem_mode != Disabled`. With the mode left at LUT the
+  // generated block preloads RBX from g_state.fastmem_base (EmitBeginBlock), which
+  // Bus::GetFastmemBase() reports as nullptr once the mode inside Bus is Disabled, and then
+  // indexes the LUT through it. Measured on hardware, in the recompiler's own RWX buffer:
+  //
+  //     fatal: rip 0000000275ecf801  addr 0000000000000000  trapno 12 err 4
+  //     bf 3c 09 00 00  mov  edi, 0x93C     ; constant guest address
+  //     89 fe           mov  esi, edi
+  //     c1 ef 0c        shr  edi, 12        ; LUT page index
+  //     81 e6 ff 0f 00 00  and esi, 0xFFF   ; offset in page
+  //     48 8b 3c fb     mov  rdi, [rbx+rdi*8]   <- faulted, rbx == 0
+  //     44 8b 24 37     mov  r12d, [rdi+rsi]
+  //
+  // ⚠ THE 12 AND THE 0xFFF ARE NOT THE HOST PAGE SIZE. HOST_PAGE_SIZE in core/types.h is
+  // swanstation's own LUT granularity over the 4 GiB guest window (FASTMEM_LUT_NUM_PAGES =
+  // 0x100000000 >> 12) and never reaches mprotect in LUT mode, so the console's 16 KiB granule
+  // is not involved here.
+  if (g_settings.cpu_fastmem_mode != CPUFastmemMode::Disabled)
+  {
+    Log_WarningPrintf("orbis: fastmem needs a SIGSEGV handler this platform cannot install; "
+                      "disabling it so the recompiler stops emitting a null base.");
+    g_settings.cpu_fastmem_mode = CPUFastmemMode::Disabled;
+  }
+  g_settings.cpu_fastmem_rewrite = false;
+#endif
+
 #if defined(__ANDROID__) && defined(__arm__) && !defined(__aarch64__) && !defined(_M_ARM64)
   if (g_settings.runahead_frames > 0)
   {
